WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:01.520
You know the scene.

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:02.520
You know the story.

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:06.240
For Tim McGraw and Faith Hill, though, it
was about way more than just playing their

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:07.240
parts.

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:09.520
Their personal reaction to what happened will
blow you away.

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:14.870
As the Yellowstone universe continues to expand,
the Western drama's first spin-off has wrapped

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:16.690
its inaugural season.

00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:22.150
1883 is a prequel to the record-breaking series
and chronicles the Dutton family's ancestors'

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:25.340
grueling journey from Texas to Montana.

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:29.750
Before the season came to an end, it was announced
that more episodes would follow — and fans

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:32.599
are already eager to see what comes after
the shocking finale.

00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:38.190
Episode 10, "This Is Not Your Heaven," sees
the Duttons' eldest daughter Elsa Dutton recovering

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:41.360
from the arrow wound she sustained in the
previous episode.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:47.320
Many 1883 fans speculated that Elsa would
survive the infection that began to set in,

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:50.060
given that she is a main character and narrates
the series.

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:53.000
However, the finale proved this theory wrong.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:57.320
Despite Elsa's condition improving as the
group pushed forward on their long journey,

00:00:57.320 --> 00:00:59.760
things suddenly took a turn for the worse.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:03.910
During "This Is Not Your Heaven," Elsa must
grapple with her doomed fate and bid an emotional

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:05.670
farewell to her family.

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:10.200
By the end of the episode, Elsa has succumbed
to the infection, declaring she is unafraid

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:13.009
before finally passing away.

00:01:13.009 --> 00:01:17.869
"I'm not scared…

00:01:17.869 --> 00:01:19.880
I'm not scared."

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:24.459
That final father-daughter scene was clearly
hard to watch for many, and 1883 stars Tim

00:01:24.459 --> 00:01:29.020
McGraw and Faith Hill have admitted that the
entire episode was difficult for them to shoot.

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:32.999
In an interview with Variety, the real-life
couple opened up about the tragic death of

00:01:32.999 --> 00:01:34.600
their on-screen daughter.

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:38.909
Describing the arc as one of the most incredible
stories she had ever read, Hill told the publication:

00:01:38.909 --> 00:01:42.549
"As a mom, I can't even allow myself to go
there.

00:01:42.549 --> 00:01:43.549
I cannot.

00:01:43.549 --> 00:01:47.789
It's really hard, even now, to talk about,
because once the tears start coming, I can't

00:01:47.789 --> 00:01:48.789
stop them.

00:01:48.789 --> 00:01:53.119
I would tell the directors, if we start going,
it's going to be hard for me to stop."

00:01:53.119 --> 00:01:57.689
While James Dutton gets to be with Elsa during
her final moments, Margaret must stay behind

00:01:57.689 --> 00:02:01.520
with their other children, watching helplessly
as her daughter is brought away to choose

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:03.079
her final resting place.

00:02:03.079 --> 00:02:06.100
This scene, in particular, shook Hill during
filming.

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:07.239
She told Variety:

00:02:07.239 --> 00:02:11.440
"Imagine knowing that your child is going
to die and you cannot be by their side?

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:13.330
I can't even imagine it ever.

00:02:13.330 --> 00:02:14.720
I have not recovered."

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:18.980
Hill went on to say that she also cannot imagine
how this loss will affect James and Margaret's

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:19.980
marriage.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:24.720
The actress has been open about how 1883 takes
an emotional toll on her, noting the death

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:28.730
of her character's sister-in-law earlier in
the season still makes her cry.

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:33.690
Meanwhile, Tim McGraw told Variety that, when
he read the last scripts for Season 1 of 1883

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:35.530
with Hill, they couldn't stop crying.

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:36.530
He said:

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:38.640
"I mean, ugly, boohoo crying.

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:40.190
I was a blubbering idiot.

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:44.681
It was just so well-written, so devastating
and heartbreaking, but at the same time, so

00:02:44.681 --> 00:02:48.100
on point and poignant for what Yellowstone
turned out to be."

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:49.100
McGraw added:

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:52.870
"It just gives you all the reasons in the
world why they fight so hard for that land

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:55.870
and why their family fights so hard to keep
what they have.

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:57.440
It just made perfect sense."

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:01.210
No matter how much sense it made, though,
he was still struck by Elsa's death.

00:03:01.210 --> 00:03:05.440
McGraw explained that he had to make an effort
not to get too emotional too early during

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:09.790
the scene — but struggled because Isabel
May looks like she could be his own daughter.

00:03:09.790 --> 00:03:13.980
"It's hard as a parent, playing that role
— you don't want to go to any dark places

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:15.510
in your mind about your kid.

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:18.950
So you have to keep trying to block that out
and still be in the moment with the character

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:21.540
that you're playing [...] but the lines do
get blurry."

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:26.700
However, he also agreed that this moment played
an important role in the greater story — even

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:28.550
if fans aren't happy about it.

00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:29.550
McGraw explained:

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:33.260
"It's going to be a good thing that they hate
that ending, because it has to be that way

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:34.490
and they know it.

00:03:34.490 --> 00:03:36.120
It's gonna be devastating.

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:39.190
Why would you watch stuff if it doesn't purge
your emotions?"

